  "de|Musik kann lauter werden (Crescendo) oder leiser verklingen (Diminuendo). Hör hin — dieser Ton wird lauter, dann leiser.": "narration/de_329db64831645994.wav",
  "de|Musik kann schneller (Accelerando) oder langsamer (Ritardando) werden. Hör hin — die Schläge rücken zusammen, dann auseinander.": "narration/de_75250fbe13077583.wav",
  "de|Musik kommt in Phrasen, wie Sätze. Diese hier steigt weg und bleibt in der Luft stehen — sie klingt wie eine FRAGE.": "narration/de_04677dde36d7e9f1.wav",
  "de|Musik steht auf fünf Linien, dem Notensystem. Noten sitzen auf den Linien und in den Zwischenräumen.": "narration/de_30f15b8332ff99c5.wav",
  "de|Musik wird durch Taktstriche in Takte geteilt. Eine Taktangabe wie 4/4 heißt: jeder Takt hat 4 Schläge.": "narration/de_3060043d02048460.wav",
  "de|Nimm auf dieselbe Weise NOCH einen Ton dazu (überspring einen, nimm den nächsten): C E G B. Das ist ein Septakkord — er klingt unruhig, als wollte er weiter.": "narration/de_50989ac53f305e79.wav",
  "de|Normalerweise teilt sich ein Schlag in ZWEI gleiche Hälften: „1-und“. Zwei Achtelnoten.": "narration/de_625ed9d1a0fcdd47.wav",
  "de|Nummeriere die Töne der Tonleiter von 1 bis 7. Auf jeder Stufe baust du einen Akkord und benennst ihn mit einer römischen Ziffer. Auf Stufe 1 von C-Dur steht C E G — Akkord I.": "narration/de_5ffa97432f8003a9.wav",
  "de|Passt eine Note nicht auf die fünf Linien, malen wir eine kleine Extra-Linie nur für sie — eine Hilfslinie. Das eingestrichene C hängt an einer, direkt unter dem Violinsystem.": "narration/de_307512d2dd2ada89.wav",
  "de|Schlagwerk wird geschlagen — Trommeln, Xylophon und Triangel. Tasteninstrumente wie Klavier und Orgel spielen viele Töne zugleich.": "narration/de_381d3bcc18b08141.wav",
  "de|Setz oben eine 3 und jeder Takt hat drei Schläge — der sanfte Schwung eines Walzers. Zähl 1-2-3, 1-2-3.": "narration/de_7c807df3fe70c277.wav",
  "de|Sing oder beatboxe deine eigene Spur — sie nimmt zwei Takte auf und wird zu einer eigenen Karte.": "narration/de_509bea4e3196f7ac.wav",
  "de|Spiel den gezeigten Ton oder schrumme mit. Je dünner die Saite, desto höher klingt sie — vom tiefen E bis zum hohen E.": "narration/de_47324ff052750a98.wav",
  "de|Stapel weitere Karten für den Groove: Drums, Bass, Akkorde, Melodie, Sparkle. Alles passt zusammen — du kannst nichts falsch machen.": "narration/de_52b7de2ea6054d7a.wav",
  "de|Statt jedes Kreuz einzeln zu schreiben, setzen wir sie einmal ganz an den Anfang — die Vorzeichen der Tonart. Sie gelten fürs ganze Stück. Das hier ist G-Dur: ein Kreuz, Fis.": "narration/de_27f35b43cd026d0f.wav",
  "de|Streicher klingen, wenn man sie streicht oder zupft: Geige, Cello, Gitarre und Harfe.": "narration/de_198bdf5f00b24f7a.wav",
  "de|Tipp eine Karte an — deine Band spielt sofort los. Das ist dein Loop, immer im Kreis.": "narration/de_35a99557fe4d5926.wav",
  "de|Tipp ✎ auf einer Karte (oder eine Note in den Noten), um zu ändern, was sie spielt — du hörst es sofort.": "narration/de_76300233e8a48c59.wav",
  "de|Trommeln spielen keine hohen und tiefen Melodien — eine Trommel macht einfach BUMM oder TSS. Drum-Noten zeigen also WELCHE Trommel und WANN, keine Tonhöhe.": "narration/de_5289635918e5c113.wav",
  "de|Töne können gebunden und weich sein (Legato) oder kurz und getrennt (Staccato). Hör hin — erst gebunden, dann kurz.": "narration/de_2905b4cafdc69cd9.wav",
  "de|Um eine Stimme zu lesen, folge nur ihrer Linie: der Sopran ist die oberste Note, der Bass die unterste. Hör hin — erst oben, dann unten.": "narration/de_34bd9702f6b7ae0d.wav",
  "de|Verzierungen sind kleine Zeichen, die eine Note schmücken. Ein Triller (tr) wechselt schnell zwischen der Note und der direkt darüber.": "narration/de_3a5b488e0c2277e3.wav",
  "de|Wenn Akkorde nach Hause → weg → nach Hause wandern, heißt diese kleine Reise zum Ruhepunkt eine Kadenz. Hör dir die ganze Reise an.": "narration/de_663540635b2856e5.wav",
  "de|Wenn du eine Note suchst, ist JEDER ihrer Plätze richtig!": "narration/de_7a874e439d92c63c.wav",
  "de|Wenn eine Melodie steigt, ist jede Note höher als die davor — im Notenbild wandern die Noten nach oben, und der Klang geht hinauf.": "narration/de_5f7a6f47c7a332e7.wav",
  "de|Wenn sie fällt, ist jede Note tiefer als die davor — die Noten wandern nach unten, und der Klang sinkt.": "narration/de_754fb8ebf669dcff.wav",
  "de|Wähl ein Lied, das du kennst. Der Bildschirm zeigt seine Melodie als Notenreihe, von links nach rechts zu lesen.": "narration/de_152048e8ab725f9f.wav",
  "de|Zu jedem Notenwert gibt es eine passende Pause. Eine halbe Note klingt 2 Schläge; eine halbe Pause ist 2 Schläge Stille.": "narration/de_7696790773af9d57.wav",
  "de|Zwei Noten mit DERSELBEN Tonhöhe klingen genau gleich — wie ein Echo. Im Notenbild stehen sie an genau derselben Stelle.": "narration/de_767e29acdaae4094.wav",
  "de|f (forte) heißt laut, ff (fortissimo) sehr laut. Dieselben Noten noch einmal — jetzt forte.": "narration/de_4d339d103dcb894a.wav",
  "de|Über einem Stück steht ein italienisches Wort für das Tempo. Largo ist sehr langsam, Adagio langsam. Hör hin — diese vier Noten sind Adagio.": "narration/de_0d33ee7c31bad8e0.wav",
  "de|Über einer Melodie stehen oft Akkordsymbole. Ein einfacher Buchstabe meint einen Dur-Akkord: „C“ sagt dir, spiel einen C-Dur-Akkord.": "narration/de_1e8d2d1bf8038067.wav",
  "de|…dann gibt sie eine ANTWORT und kommt zum Zuhause herunter, zur Ruhe. Frage und Antwort ergeben einen Satz.": "narration/de_49057a87c7a9b84e.wav",
  "de|…klingt HÖHER. Mit dem Kapo im 2. Bund klingt der C-Griff wie ein D.": "narration/de_298a1fac09c7bd81.wav",
  "de|∨ heißt AUFSTRICH: Schieb von der Spitze zurück zum Frosch. Der ist leichter — gut für Auftakte und Anläufe.": "narration/de_3a67c4c08cf1b5e1.wav",
  "de|⊓ heißt ABSTRICH: Zieh den Bogen vom Frosch (deiner Hand) zur Spitze. Das ist die schwerere Richtung — sie passt zu betonten Schlägen.": "narration/de_64fc99fbbed9572e.wav",
  "en|A SKIP jumps over one — a line straight to the next line: C to E. Skips sound bouncier than steps.": "narration/en_1ade676bdd1ad1c0.wav",
  "en|A SLUR curves over DIFFERENT pitches. Play them smoothly, joined with no gap between them — that's legato.": "narration/en_2ee5011853c2c92e.wav",
  "en|A STEP moves to the next-door note — a line to the space touching it, one letter along: C to D.": "narration/en_6d67a906f069d7e7.wav",
  "en|A TIE joins two notes of the SAME pitch. Don't play the second one — hold the first right through both. C tied to C is one long C.": "narration/en_6e6ff0ccc3eb9920.wav",
  "en|A cadence is how a phrase ends — like the end of a sentence. Ending on the HOME chord sounds finished, like a full stop. Listen: away, then home.": "narration/en_27819499f6163416.wav",
  "en|A capo clamps all the strings up a fret — like a new nut higher up the neck.": "narration/en_0f74f63c397b84b8.wav",
  "en|A choir sings four lines together: Soprano (highest), Alto, Tenor, Bass (lowest). Sounded at the same time they make a chord.": "narration/en_03ec06ba890fd0ee.wav",
  "en|A chord is notes played at the SAME time. Stack three notes a gap apart and you get a triad — here, C E G.": "narration/en_6aba26c68d537018.wav",
  "en|A different tune is a new letter — section B. Tune, different tune, then the first tune again makes the form A-B-A. Lots of songs are shaped this way!": "narration/en_284091d7bd212861.wav",
  "en|A dot above or below the notehead is staccato: play it short and detached, with air after it. (Careful — a dot BESIDE the note makes it longer instead!)": "narration/en_4c3f1b784be1640d.wav",
  "en|A dot after a note adds HALF its value again: 2 beats + 1 = a dotted half note of 3 beats. Count ‘1-2-3’.": "narration/en_61aebc03e4848246.wav",
  "en|A filled note with a stem is a quarter note — one short beat. Four quarter notes last as long as one whole note.": "narration/en_23a09605c48bd77e.wav",
  "en|A flat ♭ lowers a note by a semitone. D♭ is the very same key as C♯ — it just leans down from D.": "narration/en_7f3ad5c3b62bf63f.wav",
  "en|A guitar has six strings. From low (thick) to high (thin): E, A, D, G, B, E — yes, an E at each end!": "narration/en_1eaf22fc1b7ada65.wav",
  "en|A half note lasts 2 beats, so two half notes also fill a 4/4 measure.": "narration/en_7b3ff7b3c8d28dee.wav",
  "en|A half note lasts 2 beats. Count ‘1-2’ while it rings.": "narration/en_1de81ba450708b47.wav",
  "en|A half step (semitone) is the smallest step on the keyboard — neighbours with nothing between. E to F is a half step: no black key between them.": "narration/en_4e796d9999b6ffa6.wav",
  "en|A lone eighth note wears a flag on its stem. Here a rest splits the eighths apart, so each one keeps its own flag.": "narration/en_1eb06eed2616a623.wav",
  "en|A major scale sounds bright and cheerful. Listen to C major climb up.": "narration/en_64d8a5f3915323e1.wav",
  "en|A major scale sounds bright and happy. Listen to it climb.": "narration/en_37604fa37e45ada3.wav",
  "en|A major triad sounds bright; a minor triad sounds softer and sadder. Listen to both.": "narration/en_6142b7b8157cb189.wav",
  "en|A marker slides along the tune. Sing or play each note as it reaches the line — like following a bouncing ball.": "narration/en_64d7a6d48d0c9526.wav",
  "en|A melody is a little journey of notes — some step up, some come down. Hum along as it rises and falls!": "narration/en_4a836c4eb3623db9.wav",
  "en|A minor scale sounds darker — its 3rd, 6th and 7th steps sit a little lower.": "narration/en_0785211e9113689b.wav",
  "en|A minor scale sounds darker, a little sad. Listen to A minor.": "narration/en_1fc655d630cf8fd2.wav",
  "en|A note's shape shows how LONG to hold it. This open oval with no stem is a whole note — a long sound (4 beats).": "narration/en_242f144769bda73b.wav",
  "en|A rest is a beat of SILENCE. Count it in your head, but don't play. Tap Listen to hear a beat of rest.": "narration/en_7d8b86549336cb9a.wav",
  "en|A rest is silence — and you count it, just like a note. Here it goes play, rest, play, rest — one beat each.": "narration/en_60799c4e592b2180.wav",
  "en|A scale is a ladder of notes climbing step by step. This is C major: C D E F G A B C.": "narration/en_2044dd59364884f1.wav",
  "en|A sharp ♯ in front of a note lifts it up by the smallest step, a semitone. C♯ is a hair higher than C.": "narration/en_7e0d607dd2f12166.wav",
  "en|A small ‘m’ after the letter means minor: ‘Am’ is A minor — the same family, but a softer, sadder colour.": "narration/en_058ad46a83abeed8.wav",
  "en|A triad is three notes — take one, skip one, take the next, skip one: C, E, G. That is C major, and it sounds settled.": "narration/en_6789dbfdc941cd0b.wav",
  "en|A triplet squeezes THREE equal notes into that same beat: \"trip-o-let\". It gets a little 3 above it.": "narration/en_04a15c0df2e24ac5.wav",
  "en|A tune can ask a QUESTION — it stops high up in the air, sounding unfinished, as if it's waiting.": "narration/en_547c43dd2369af0b.wav",
  "en|A tune has a home note. Here it climbs and comes back to the same home both times — it stays in one key.": "narration/en_6462a70b49a2bfa2.wav",
  "en|A turn (a sideways S) curls AROUND the note: the note above, the note, the note below, then back. A mordent is just one quick flick up and back.": "narration/en_2e37d523e1e9e4fd.wav",
  "en|A wedge > is an accent: give that note an extra push so it stands out from its neighbours.": "narration/en_0d1c116f9af49411.wav",
  "en|A whole step is two half steps. C to D is a whole step — there IS a black key between them.": "narration/en_272dded4ebbb1c23.wav",
  "en|Above a tune you'll see chord symbols. A plain letter means a major chord: ‘C’ tells you to play a C major chord.": "narration/en_45517285881f340a.wav",
  "en|Add one MORE note the same way (skip one, take the next) and you get a seventh chord: C E G B♭. It sounds restless — as if it wants to move on.": "narration/en_0d710174502a5baf.wav",
  "en|Allegro is fast, Presto very fast. The same four notes, just quicker — listen to the difference.": "narration/en_74a00b377290e213.wav",
  "en|An upbeat (or pickup) starts with a note or two BEFORE the first barline, leading into beat 1. Listen — the tune leans in.": "narration/en_7b912915ff9f8d53.wav",
  "en|At the top of a piece an Italian word gives the speed. Largo is very slow, Adagio is slow. Listen — these four notes are Adagio.": "narration/en_58d33da2c9622df6.wav",
  "en|But some are ‘transposing’: a trumpet in B♭ reads a C yet a B♭ comes out — a little lower. This game does that swap for you.": "narration/en_00aec0a49bf3e599.wav",
  "en|C major has no sharps or flats at all. Listen to C major — every note is a plain white key.": "narration/en_455ee345e33fd169.wav",
  "en|CAPITALS mean a major chord (I, IV, V); small letters mean minor (ii, iii, vi). On step 2 of C major sits D F A — chord ii, D minor.": "narration/en_7c1f7453e7fb04c2.wav",
  "en|Cello notes live on the bass clef, the staff for low sounds. This deep note is C, your thickest string.": "narration/en_68da1b6c80f4c3cf.wav",
  "en|Cellos and trombones use it for their higher notes — it keeps them on the staff instead of piling up ledger lines above the bass clef.": "narration/en_0147549ea84baaf2.wav",
  "en|Change the top number to 3 and each measure has three beats — the gentle swing of a waltz. Count 1-2-3, 1-2-3.": "narration/en_29bc43e78fc20bc1.wav",
  "en|Dorian is like minor, but its 6th step is raised — so it sounds minor with a brighter twist. That one note is the whole secret!": "narration/en_595348dd6053af49.wav",
  "en|Drums don't play high and low tunes — a drum just goes THUMP or TSS. So drum music shows WHICH drum and WHEN, not a pitch.": "narration/en_6dc3cc03c1d18ff2.wav",
  "en|Dynamics tell you how loud to play. p (piano) means soft — and pp (pianissimo) is very soft. Listen: this is piano.": "narration/en_223d0150afe98791.wav",
  "en|Each line and space is a different drum: low down is the bass drum you kick, higher up are the snare and cymbals. Read left to right and play the beat.": "narration/en_0485c3e5c993dbd7.wav",
  "en|Ending on a different chord leaves it hanging, like a question mark — your ear expects more to come. Listen: home, then away.": "narration/en_02ede36e65b59f80.wav",
  "en|Every note has a letter name: A B C D E F G. This one is E — tap Listen to hear it.": "narration/en_58f79d11aff5792a.wav",
  "en|Every note value has a matching rest. A half note rings for 2 beats; a half rest is 2 beats of silence.": "narration/en_676196775e95d27d.wav",
  "en|Expression is HOW you play. One part is the speed (tempo): listen to this phrase slow, then fast.": "narration/en_3f4fc9c94ce5c5d7.wav",
  "en|Fill each measure so the beats add up. Four quarter notes make 4 beats — one full 4/4 measure.": "narration/en_348456a3f8a4d436.wav",
  "en|Find C: it's the white key just to the LEFT of every group of TWO black keys. From C, climb up C D E F G A B C.": "narration/en_417de9a80ab36a70.wav",
  "en|Fine marks the end. Dal Segno (D.S.) jumps back to the sign, and a Coda is a special ending section you leap to. Match each sign to what it does!": "narration/en_6fe5844735330fb0.wav",
  "en|Guitar can be written as tab: six lines, one per string. A number is the fret to press; 0 means play the open string.": "narration/en_6cf266ecde0ec661.wav",
  "en|If the second note is even one step higher or lower, it is different — and it sits somewhere else on the staff. Listen: C then D.": "narration/en_0fbbd6148f0b0add.wav",
  "en|In 4/4 you count 1-2-3-4 over and over. Beat 1 is the STRONG beat — the one you tap hardest. Beats 2, 3 and 4 are lighter.": "narration/en_64c925a15538af95.wav",
  "en|In CLOSE position the top three voices are bunched together — the highest and the tenor sit within one octave.": "narration/en_2a790d42bd512594.wav",
  "en|In OPEN position the top voices are spread out — the highest note is more than an octave above the tenor.": "narration/en_0f165dc13ae4af62.wav",
  "en|Instead of marking every sharp, we write them once at the very start — a key signature. It applies to the whole piece. This is G major: one sharp, F♯.": "narration/en_4e3bae9edf8a5ec8.wav",
  "en|Listen and count how many separate notes go by. Here there are three — count one for each new sound.": "narration/en_241f382b40e92ac5.wav",
  "en|Listen. Is this getting louder or softer?": "narration/en_3a53849a84724145.wav",
  "en|Listen. Is this speeding up or slowing down?": "narration/en_2ed85d270c7c1e0a.wav",
  "en|Middle C — the note in the middle of the piano — sits just above the bass staff, on its own little ledger line.": "narration/en_1384e138515a0cf5.wav",
  "en|Most instruments sound the note they read. Read a C, hear a C — simple.": "narration/en_6c794276aa504ea1.wav",
  "en|Most tunes start on beat 1 — the strong downbeat. Count ‘1-2-3-4’ and begin on the 1.": "narration/en_17fdfa84aa6397e0.wav",
  "en|Music can grow louder (a crescendo) or fade away softer (a diminuendo). Listen — this note gets louder, then softer.": "narration/en_0bd6cfba51cd2a32.wav",
  "en|Music can speed up (accelerando) or slow down (ritardando). Listen — the beats get closer, then further apart.": "narration/en_4b0017871f51aa30.wav",
  "en|Music comes in phrases, like sentences. This one climbs away and stops in the air — it sounds like a QUESTION.": "narration/en_4091cdba41ee7649.wav",
  "en|Music is built from sections. Here is a little tune — call it section A. Whenever it comes back, it is A again.": "narration/en_3ddf5bbea13dbd8a.wav",
  "en|Music is split into measures (bars) by barlines. A time signature like 4/4 means each measure holds 4 beats.": "narration/en_345ee4c36a0a0f20.wav",
  "en|Music is written on five lines called a staff. Notes sit on the lines and in the spaces between them.": "narration/en_3f7517f56f6f4d3e.wav",
  "en|Narrow intervals sound close and gentle; wide ones sound open and bold. Listen to a small gap, then a big one.": "narration/en_64c5ead9f833b25a.wav",
  "en|Normally a beat splits into TWO even halves: \"1-and\". Two eighth notes.": "narration/en_41e8d33ff2574d6e.wav",
  "en|Notes can be smooth and connected (legato) or short and detached (staccato). Listen — smooth first, then short.": "narration/en_59ef8544cf6b12c8.wav",
  "en|Now four. They come quickly, so count each one the moment it arrives.": "narration/en_667f3401e01089b0.wav",
  "en|Now listen: are these two notes the same or different?": "narration/en_4d2173f52acc7943.wav",
  "en|Now listen: how many notes do you hear?": "narration/en_6a87c882f178a82a.wav",
  "en|Now you try: E to F — whole step or half step?": "narration/en_337c8cb4a99df96a.wav",
  "en|Now you try: are these upper voices close or open?": "narration/en_76a2d7e029ea594a.wav",
  "en|Now you try: count the steps from C up to E (C-D-E). What number is it?": "narration/en_2dccae789d28abc3.wav",
  "en|Now you try: do F♯ and G♭ sound the same or different?": "narration/en_53501c5976232320.wav",
  "en|Now you try: dots over the notes mean…?": "narration/en_13f72e7eee612f08.wav",
  "en|Now you try: how many beats are in a 3/4 measure?": "narration/en_6af4a07127c3e20b.wav",
  "en|Now you try: how many beats does this whole note last?": "narration/en_3ccab55cd88b6047.wav",
  "en|Now you try: how many beats fill this 4/4 measure?": "narration/en_24156637feb8ddbf.wav",
  "en|Now you try: how many different notes does a major scale have before it repeats?": "narration/en_1cc0b9f91ca139f7.wav",
  "en|Now you try: how many notes are in a seventh chord?": "narration/en_09f04f147e467540.wav",
  "en|Now you try: how many notes build a triad (a basic chord)?": "narration/en_3a020beda78d5fc8.wav",
  "en|Now you try: how many sharps does C major have?": "narration/en_787dc6ff867b48c2.wav",
  "en|Now you try: in 4/4, which beat is the strongest?": "narration/en_3e63552b3d7253ba.wav",
  "en|Now you try: is this a step or a skip?": "narration/en_475e40a5b3525e69.wav",
  "en|Now you try: same pitch under a curve — tie or slur?": "narration/en_6728e86c869f28de.wav",
  "en|Now you try: two eighths joined on one beat are…?": "narration/en_25a7b14464fea1d5.wav",
  "en|Now you try: what letter name is this note?": "narration/en_5024f8a9a52cb106.wav",
  "en|Now you try: which sign RAISES a note?": "narration/en_28986f9bee7fde8b.wav",
  "en|Now you try: which way does this pair go?": "narration/en_4dd61e1ed5d3c6ca.wav",
  "en|Number the notes of the scale 1 to 7. Build a chord on each step and name it with a Roman numeral. On step 1 of C major sits C E G — chord I.": "narration/en_4881d3ecbe6adc82.wav",
  "en|One chord feels like HOME — settled and finished. We call it the Tonic. Listen: this is C major, home base.": "narration/en_0ca28282393d322d.wav",
  "en|Ornaments are little signs that dress up a note. A trill (tr) shakes quickly between the note and the one just above it.": "narration/en_004105e00d16f8d6.wav",
  "en|Other chords pull AWAY and want to return home. The Dominant tugs the hardest — hear how it leans right back to home.": "narration/en_27f48a33b97291d2.wav",
  "en|Percussion is struck — drums, xylophone and triangle. Keyboards like the piano and organ play many notes at once.": "narration/en_00ba0c4fbef112a6.wav",
  "en|Piano music uses two staves at once: the top staff for your right hand, the bottom staff for your left. Hear both together.": "narration/en_350f15255b7645fb.wav",
  "en|Pick a song you know. The screen shows its tune as a line of notes, read left to right.": "narration/en_61bc5338c6e28b41.wav",
  "en|Play the note shown, or strum along. The thinner the string, the higher it sings — from low E up to high E.": "narration/en_343cc333e837c51f.wav",
  "en|Press a finger onto a string to shorten it and the note gets higher. The tuner listens and shows if you're spot on.": "narration/en_432dc2914e44e83f.wav",
  "en|Sing or Beatbox your own part — it records over two bars and joins the band as its own card.": "narration/en_557ea1f4dbf61058.wav",
  "en|So F♯ and G♭ sound identical. Other twins: C♯=D♭, D♯=E♭, G♯=A♭, A♯=B♭.": "narration/en_70e6554fe5464942.wav",
  "en|So a shape you know, like C…": "narration/en_5514375c39d79c58.wav",
  "en|So when you look for a note, tapping ANY of its spots is right!": "narration/en_3986d0de1a1e6314.wav",
  "en|Some signs tell you where to go. Da Capo (D.C.) means \"from the top\" — jump back to the very beginning and play again.": "narration/en_0c9610fce666f2b7.wav",
  "en|Stack more cards to build the groove: drums, bass, chords, melody, sparkle. Everything fits, so you can't go wrong.": "narration/en_0dda51b281468477.wav",
  "en|Strings sing when you bow or pluck them: the violin, cello, guitar and harp.": "narration/en_79d0427c6e630895.wav",
  "en|Stuck? Roll 🎲 for a surprise. Want more? Open the beat in the Drum Kit, or the whole song in the Tracker or Score Workshop.": "narration/en_20d28fb72a495f98.wav",
  "en|Syncopation pushes notes OFF the beat, onto the \"and\" in between. The accent lands where your ear didnt expect it — thats the kick you feel in pop and jazz.": "narration/en_4c203859e99531fd.wav",
  "en|Tap a card — your band starts playing the instant you do. That's your loop, going round and round.": "narration/en_490e2d840dbe1c42.wav",
  "en|Tap ✎ on any card (or a note on the sheet music) to change what it plays — you hear the change right away.": "narration/en_172278976f2b4712.wav",
  "en|The answering phrase comes back to the note the tune started from — its home note. That is why it sounds finished.": "narration/en_42406c5db05adc45.wav",
  "en|The bass clef (F-clef) puts two dots around the line that means F. It is used for lower notes — left hand, cello, bass.": "narration/en_4aede1ca8a7f89fa.wav",
  "en|The bottom one is bass — usually your left hand. Middle C sits in the gap between the two staves, on its own little ledger line.": "narration/en_13bc75bc8c936f67.wav",
  "en|The cello has four strings. From low to high they are C, G, D, A — the lowest string is the thickest.": "narration/en_550661730fb4c858.wav",
  "en|The distance between two notes is an interval. Count the letters including both ends: C to E is C-D-E — a 3rd.": "narration/en_37ab7e018adef3e3.wav",
  "en|The higher a note climbs above the staff, the more ledger lines it needs. Count them like the rungs of a ladder.": "narration/en_4d68ef0927754707.wav",
  "en|The higher a note sits on the staff, the higher it sounds. Tap Listen and hear these notes climb!": "narration/en_3d7bc5f9b1706f16.wav",
  "en|The meter decides which beat is strong. In 3/4 you count 1-2-3, and the 1 is strong again — that's the lilt of a waltz.": "narration/en_3cadd2a0deeb4f97.wav",
  "en|The other part is how loud (dynamics): the same phrase soft (p), then loud (f). Charades asks you to name what you heard.": "narration/en_46f5b59e7d0f6e0b.wav",
  "en|The piano writes on a GRAND STAFF: two staves joined by a brace. The top one is treble — usually your right hand.": "narration/en_6d048908f0bd7b5b.wav",
  "en|The same note lives in several spots on the fretboard — different strings, different frets.": "narration/en_0a96d7d467b35b2f.wav",
  "en|The tenor clef is a C-clef: the middle of the sign points straight at middle C. Wherever the sign sits, that line IS middle C.": "narration/en_40cf2502cbcd52c7.wav",
  "en|The treble clef (G-clef) curls around the line that means G. It is used for higher notes — right hand, flute, violin.": "narration/en_3c6d7fb4cc7942b6.wav",
  "en|The two numbers at the start are the time signature. The top number is how many beats fill each measure — 4 means a steady four.": "narration/en_5ece14b71d7c0e69.wav",
  "en|The white keys are named A B C D E F G, repeating up the whole piano. The black keys sit in little groups of two and three.": "narration/en_19aaa10af425db91.wav",
  "en|The wider the gap, the bigger the number. C up to G is a 5th: C-D-E-F-G.": "narration/en_2c629773dac32dcf.wav",
  "en|This low staff is the bass clef (the F-clef). A cello or a left hand reads here. Its lines and spaces spell different notes than the treble clef.": "narration/en_4b60e0bd2147ce4f.wav",
  "en|This piano key can be written as F♯ or G♭ — the very same sound, spelled two ways. They are ‘enharmonic’ twins.": "narration/en_12c9d3cfefa49891.wav",
  "en|This time the second half is lifted higher, landing on a new home note. The music has changed key — that is modulation.": "narration/en_655c195caad349be.wav",
  "en|To read one voice, follow only its line: the soprano is the top note, the bass the bottom. Listen — top voice, then bottom.": "narration/en_2462d44f77814233.wav",
  "en|Two notes at the SAME pitch sound exactly alike — like an echo. On the staff they sit in the very same place.": "narration/en_7d85b4e942867470.wav",
  "en|Usually the notes land right ON the beats — count 1-2-3-4 and each note lands on a number. Steady and square.": "narration/en_0beea5f9b0f2be24.wav",
  "en|When a melody climbs, every note is higher than the one before — on the staff the notes walk upward, and the sound rises.": "narration/en_44a224b70582ebdb.wav",
  "en|When a note won't fit on the five lines, we add a tiny extra line just for it — a ledger line. Middle C hangs on one, right below the treble staff.": "narration/en_494e3a50189b29ba.wav",
  "en|When chords travel home → away → home, that little journey to a resting point is a cadence. Listen to the whole trip.": "narration/en_149f2ab4c653558d.wav",
  "en|When eighths share a beat they are joined by a BEAM instead of flags — the same sound, just tidier to read.": "narration/en_64e0fd27abd64bd4.wav",
  "en|When it falls, every note is lower than the one before — the notes walk down the staff, and the sound sinks.": "narration/en_3c15d079799437c7.wav",
  "en|Winds need your breath. Woodwinds like the flute, clarinet and saxophone; brass like the trumpet, trombone and tuba.": "narration/en_5886ccb5c8571e14.wav",
  "en|You already know intervals from songs! A cuckoos call — “Kuck-uck” — is a falling minor 3rd. “Alle meine Entchen” starts with a major 2nd going up.": "narration/en_66f82b2171c97738.wav",
  "en|You can also play a chord one note at a time, bottom to top — that's an arpeggio.": "narration/en_428b66885e529261.wav",
  "en|f (forte) means loud, and ff (fortissimo) very loud. The same notes again — now forte.": "narration/en_00b001bb97f1592e.wav",
  "en|…sounds HIGHER. With the capo on the 2nd fret, that C shape rings out as a D.": "narration/en_232c1be4d591fca1.wav",
  "en|…then it gives an ANSWER, coming back down to rest at home. A question and its answer make a phrase.": "narration/en_6d71e8f1262fadfc.wav",
  "en|∨ means UP-bow: push from the tip back toward the frog. It's lighter — good for upbeats and lead-ins.": "narration/en_6a7f5f8c3e2e5bc3.wav",
  "en|⊓ means DOWN-bow: pull the bow from the frog (your hand) toward the tip. It's the heavier direction, so it suits strong beats.": "narration/en_7f7795dd7e02437b.wav"
}